<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pieds-noirs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The pieds-noirs were people of European origin who settled in French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> during colonial rule. More than a million lived there by the mid-twentieth century, dominating export agriculture while indigenous Algerians faced legal discrimination. Most left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at Algerian independence in 1962, a defining episode in the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pieds-noirs.jpg" alt="Pieds-noirs" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo familiale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%A9r%C3%A9monie_de_communion_solennelle_de_pieds-noirs_%C3%A0_Sidi-Bel-Abb%C3%A8s,_d%C3%A9but_du_XXe_si%C3%A8cle.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The pieds-noirs are people of French and other European descent who were born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> during French rule from 1830 to 1962. European settlement began in the 1830s, after the French seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1830 and began the conquest. The Dey of Algiers had struck the French consul with a fly-swatter in 1827, though economic reasons are also cited. The French met long resistance from Emir Abd al-Kader and forced his surrender in 1847. In 1848 Algeria was divided into three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, Alger, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> and Constantine, and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The settlers often took land forcibly taken from the local population. Nearly half of the settlers of the 1880s came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, and the rest were mostly poor French.</p><p>The term pied-noir came into common use shortly before the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a> in 1962. Its origin is disputed: one theory points to sailors working barefoot in ships' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> rooms, another to the black boots of French soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The pieds-noirs were about 10 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s population from the later nineteenth century to independence, peaking at 15.2 percent in 1926. In 1959 they numbered 1,025,000, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> metropolitan area they were 49.3 percent of the population. They included peasants and large landowners, the latter called grands colons. The settlers had full political representation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but Muslim Algerians had none in the National Assembly until 1945, and to obtain citizenship they had to renounce their Muslim identity, so that only about 2,500 did so before 1930. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Algeria received French citizenship through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cremieux-decree">Crémieux Decree</a> of 1870, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime abolished and the Free French restored in 1943, and came to be counted as part of the community.</p><p>Many pieds-noirs felt alienated from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>: 28 percent had never visited France. During the Algerian War, which began in 1954, a vast majority supported French rule and opposed the National Liberation Front.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After independence in 1962 about 800,000 pieds-noirs of French nationality evacuated to mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and about 200,000 remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, of whom there were about 100,000 in 1965 and 30,000 in 1993. In France they met ostracism from some left-wing movements for their perceived exploitation of Muslims, and others blamed them for the war and the collapse of the Fourth Republic. Their history is marked by a double alienation, from the land of their birth and from their adopted homeland. Some express a feeling of nostalgia for French Algeria, called nostalgérie. The community adopted as an unofficial anthem Captain Félix Boyer's 1943 version of Le Chant des Africains, which was banned as official military music from 1962 until August 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pieds-noirs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>European settlers came from France, Spain, Italy and Malta; a law of 1889 made the children of foreign Europeans born in Algeria French, and by 1954 there were about a million Europeans, later called <b>pieds-noirs</b>, beside some eight to nine million Muslim Algerians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Algeria, organised into French departments from 1848, attracted around one million European settlers, the <b>pieds-noirs</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Decolonisation also reversed the flow of migration: most of the <b>pieds-noirs</b> left Algeria for France in 1962, and some 800,000 people moved to Portugal from its African colonies after 1974.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
